<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antipater</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 397–319 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antipater was a Macedonian general and statesman who served Philip II and governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. After Alexander's death he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and its allies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamian-war">Lamian War</a> at Crannon in 322 BCE. He was regent of the empire from 321 BCE until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antipater.jpg" alt="Antipater" width="320" height="159" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KINGS_of_MACEDON._Alexander_III_%27the_Great%27._336-323_BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…411 BCE and by the Thirty Tyrants installed by Sparta in 404 BCE, but was restored in 403 BCE and lasted, with pay for attending the Assembly and a code of laws revised by legislative boards, until the Macedonian regent <b>Antipater</b> imposed a property qualification in 322 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Hellenistic age it gave the Greek world <b>Antipater</b> of Sidon, the poet, Zeno of Sidon, the Epicurean philosopher, Dorotheus of Sidon, the astrologer, and Boethus of Sidon, the Peripatetic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It stayed out of the League of Corinth, and King Agis III's revolt against Macedon was crushed by <b>Antipater</b> at Megalopolis in 331 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Restored in 403 BCE, it lasted until 322 BCE, when <b>Antipater</b>, the Macedonian regent, having defeated Athens in the Lamian War, limited citizenship to men owning at least 2,000 drachmas.</blockquote><a href="/government/direct-democracy">Direct democracy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>The first well-documented direct democracy was that of Athens, from the reforms of Cleisthenes in 508–507 BCE until 322 BCE, when the Macedonian regent <b>Antipater</b> limited citizenship to men owning at least 2,000 drachmas.</blockquote><a href="/essays/who-should-rule">Who Should Rule?</a></li><li><blockquote>After the regent <b>Antipater</b> died in 319 BCE, his son Cassander fought Polyperchon and Olympias for Macedon and won control by 316 BCE, executing Olympias.</blockquote><a href="/state/antipatrid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antipatrid Kingdom of Macedonia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Paliura. Died in Macedonia. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
